  <reporting-org ref="GB-CHC-285776" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CAFOD</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promotion of peaceful co-existence</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mannar District, in the north-west, is one of the least developed areas of Sri Lanka.  Ethnically it is predominantly Tamil and Tamil-speaking, but religiously it is very mixed (Hindus, very high proportion of Catholics, other Christian groups including &#x201c;new churches&#x201d;, and Muslims).  For over 20 years it was severely affected by civil conflict, with frequent population displacements, both long- and short-term, into, out of, and within the district; control of territory was disputed between government and LTTE armed forces, with other armed groups also operating.  Besides disruption of family life and threats to personal security, the damage to livelihood opportunities contributed to a considerable rise in mistrust and social tension.  Resources for Peace and Reconciliation works basically by forming Village Reconciliation Committees, which are then provided with skills training in mediation, non-violent communication etc, and support in carrying out peace building and conflict resolution at village level.</narrative>
  </description>
